in in the in the context of the  retinal lesion experiment what we do is  make  several injections of the of the virus  and along a line  of injection sites and then after  um and then we we can image the pattern  of projections  uh from the injection site into one side  or  or another of the line and then after  getting a baseline map  of the circuit in the in the visual  cortex we make a retinal lesion where  the lesion boundary  is placed right to one side of that uh  instead of  injection so that the projections to  this side  correspond to the lesion projection zone  and the lesion  and the the image the the projections to  this side  correspond to projections in normal  visual cortex  so first of all this shows you what the  two photon images look like so this is  in the living brain  uh you can this is in the injection site  you can you can make out  the spines from the the dendrites of  neurons  and you can see these clusters of axon  collaterals i notice this sort of  blebbing  along the the course of the axon so  these are known as axonal boutons  and they're the site of presynaptic uh  these are the pre-synaptic sites along  the axon  now when we don't do any lesion just to  look look from week to week  at the at these axonal boutons we see  that they are constantly disappearing  and appearing at a rate of about seven  percent per week  so this is this rather extraordinary uh  phenomenon  that the the the circuits within our  brains are not fixed but the synapses  are constantly undergoing this churning  um throughout life and we don't know  whether there may be some synapses that  are more relatively stable but at least  over the period of time that we imaged  we saw this turnover  occurring at that rate you'd see you  know over three weeks 21  new boutons and and 21 disappearing  but but but at least but but without  making a lesion  the branching pattern seems to be  relatively stable  so this is what we see when we make a  retinal lesion and look in the lesion  projection zone  so again we've made an injection to one  side and we're looking at the  lesion projection zone at different time  points before  on the day of and after we make the  lesion  and what was quite extraordinary is that  on the same day that we make the lesion  we see this extraordinary outgrowth of  new axons  so these yellow traced axons are the  ones that have grown  relative to the previous imaging point  and the red ones are the ones that  disappear  uh relative to the previous imaging  point and you can see that on a week by  week basis  there is constant new outgrowth and  pruning  occurring in parallel with a net  increase in the density of these  horizontal connections in the lesion  projection zone  over the the imaging period and we think  this  then corresponds very closely to what we  see in terms like  when we record with these arrays to the  rather rapid initial  uh uh change in the in the receptive  fields of neurons that we see on the  same  day as we make in the lesion and the  eventual remapping of the visual cortex  that occurs  uh invading this the central of the  towards towards the center of the lesion  projection zone  and we can compare what happens to these  excitatory  connections to what happens with  inhibitory connections  by using a virus that that solumeric had  constructed  that is under the control of the gad65  promoter so we can then selectively  image  inhibitory neurons and this just shows  that  by by co-labeling uh  neurons with the gad 65 aav virus  and a cocktail of calcium binding  proteins which  labels 99 of inhibitory neurons that we  see co-localization  uh and therefore we think that we are  selectively labeling inhibitory neurons  and this shows that you get a similar  sort of  efflorescence and pruning of exons of  inhibitory neurons within the lesion  projection zone  we have made a similar series of  experience experiments in a mouse model  that allows us to more uh  take to take a detailed look at the  molecular mechanisms underlying this  plasticity  and so uh this one is one involving the  whisker barrel system of the mouth so  the the the the positions of the  whiskers  are mapped in the somatosensory cortex  of the mouse  in terms of a series of rows of barrels  and what  and one can induce plasticity and we can  create a lesion projection zone  by plucking the whiskers so we pluck the  d and e rows of whiskers  and what happens is that over a period  of time these  these barrels which are initially  silenced then become responsive to input  from the c  row of whiskers so there's again a  remapping that is taking place  and we can see that this remapping is  associated with a sprouting  of excitatory connections into the  deprived barrels  and a sprouting of inhibitory neurons  whose axons are within the  who are resident within their private  barrels towards their the source of this  new excitatory input which we think is  useful for  maintaining a balance between excitation  so here's the  balance of excitation inhibition so  here's the sprouting of excitatory input  into the barrels  and here's the sprouting of inhibitory  connections from the barrels  and and we can then see what the  molecular mechanism may be and this is  something we've seen both in the monkey  visual cortex following retinal lesions  and in the mouse somatosensory cortex  following whisker plucking that these  these orange  labeled neurons are ones expressing bdnf  and the brain derived neurotrophic  factor  and so this and these are local  localized  to the deprived barrels and we think  therefore is the stimulus for the  sprouting of connections into the lesion  projection zone  but also keep in mind that we see this  phenomenon of pruning of connections  and this is and for this we've looked  uh from after being inspired by some of  the work of mark tessie levine  we've looked at the apoptotic pathway so  a  a signaling pathway that is involved  in cell death early in development  does not lead to cell death in the adult  but  as i'll show you is responsible for  pruning of connections  so and this is where it's useful to have  a mouse model from time to time until  uh until bob comes along with the  the monkey knockouts that we need um  if we knock out uh so there's there's an  interaction between the the  neurotrophin signaling pathway and  a receptor known as death receptor six  this guy here  and that leads to the activation of  caspases  which then leads to accidental  retraction so if we knock out  death receptor 6 so this is what we see  normally  there's this sprouting of new  connections in yellow pruning of old  connections  in red that occurs progressively from  day to day  in the mouse somatosensory cortex but  that pruning entirely disappears in the  back's knockout  and so it appears that this pathway may  be  useful for uh the sculpting of  connections  and interestingly we see what seems to  be even a heightened  outgrowth of connections when we make  these knockouts along the um  along this pathway so um  so i to summarize um  there seems to be different levels of  dynamics or plasticity  that occurs within the visual cortex and  this is seen not just in higher order  cortical  areas that are thought to represent  declarative memory but  is a dynamic process that can even  affect neurons at the very earliest  stages  of visual cortical processing and one  may be concerned that if the primary  sensory area  is so dynamic why doesn't that disrupt  your ability  to uh to see things in a stable visual  environment  and the answer to that is because of the  fact that the the the alterations in  function  are only seen when you're doing the  train task  so these properties are just available  in a just-in-time  fashion that that they that when you're  doing  a contour detection task or a three line  bisection task the neuron  the the learned properties are manifest  um  only when you're doing the task so that  this this top down interaction  is required for for both the encoding of  the learned information  and for its recall and  uh since uh i'm in this this context of  a general lecture  i like to speculate more broadly that  this may  raise a general theory of of brain  interactions of the circuit mechanisms  of brain function  that every cortical area  has represented within it an association  field  and by these top-down interactions  neurons can select components of their  association fields and take on different  functional roles  in ways that are appropriate for the  task at hand  suppressing task irrelevant information  and  gating allowing the input from task  relevant information  and this idea of interactions between  global cortical circuits and local  circuits  this idea that feedback gates the local  circuits is something that may operate  not just in cortico-cortical  interactions  but in interactions between the  hippocampus and the in the cortex where  the hippocampus can be thought of  as being a pointer for linking bits of  information across the  cortical maps where there  it can be used for more rapid learning  but also  its role can be decoupled after  consolidation  and for interactions between the stride  the striatum  and the say the motor cortex or or  prefrontal cortex  where again the street item would serve  a pointer function  for sequencing operations that are  occurred that  that occur with with habit learning and  so  we think this may be a sort of a useful  theoretical framework  to think about how circuits can  contribute to  cortical function and for the dynamics  of cortical function  and so i just wanted to acknowledge a  number of my collaborators this is  sort of historically long list but  in particular woo lee has been had  pioneered in doing some of the most  wonderful  experiments where we look at the  properties of neurons  in the monkey in different behavioral  contexts  pike had done this very nice  computational model which we hope to  extend  in the future to see how we can imagine  how the dynamics  in input selection can occur for more  specific tasks  and uh of course uh torsten was involved  early on  in identifying the this important class  of connections which we think  are playing a critical role in mediating  the association field  and its gating and we have um  enjoyed the the input from gerald  westheimer and doing  the the psychophysical experiments from  winfred dank  and designing and building a new uh  two-photon imaging system and shimon  almond for some of the modeling work  that we've done so it's it's really  it's sort of a it  it takes a community to actually  successfully pursue these  these experiments that have to be done  in a in a in a highly collaborative and  interdisciplinary way  so i thank you all for for coming and  open for questions
 

